WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.510
“Maths test moved to Thursday.”

2
00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:04.150
…

3
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:07.240
It wrote “mass chest”?

4
00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:08.280
Again!

5
00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:10.850
My colleague says the same

6
00:00:10.850 --> 00:00:13.350
words — perfect, first time.

7
00:00:13.970 --> 00:00:16.550
So what’s wrong with my voice?

8
00:00:18.010 --> 00:00:21.030
Today: why AI can work better for some

9
00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:23.810
people than others — and how to fix it.

10
00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:27.090
Nothing is wrong with Salma’s voice.

11
00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:29.320
Voice typing learned from

12
00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:30.480
recordings of speech.

13
00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:34.360
If most came from a few accents, it works

14
00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:36.860
best for voices like those — and makes

15
00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:39.140
more mistakes with voices that were rare.

16
00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:42.420
This happens with real tools.

17
00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:46.380
In a 2020 US study, speech tools from

18
00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:49.800
five big companies got about 35 words

19
00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:53.060
wrong in 100 for Black speakers, and

20
00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:54.540
19 for white speakers.

21
00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:58.500
When an AI system works worse for

22
00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:00.020
some groups of people than for

23
00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:01.880
others, we call it bias.

24
00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:05.380
It often starts with a data gap: some

25
00:01:05.380 --> 00:01:08.800
people, places, accents or languages are

26
00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:10.760
missing or rare in the examples.

27
00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:13.530
Think of marking homework.

28
00:01:14.390 --> 00:01:17.010
A new pupil writes her ones with a long

29
00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:19.970
top stroke, so you read them as sevens.

30
00:01:20.450 --> 00:01:22.630
You weren’t unfair — you’d

31
00:01:22.630 --> 00:01:23.650
never seen that style.

32
00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:26.470
A few examples later, it’s easy.

33
00:01:27.830 --> 00:01:28.410
Quick check!

34
00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:32.040
A malaria app learned mostly

35
00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:33.960
from one kind of parasite.

36
00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:36.830
Where will it make more mistakes?

37
00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:41.070
Other kinds — and it’s a real case.

38
00:01:41.830 --> 00:01:45.250
In Uganda, Makerere University researchers

39
00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:48.410
are testing a malaria tool trained mainly

40
00:01:48.410 --> 00:01:50.230
on the most common parasite there.

41
00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:53.380
It needs more examples to find the others.

42
00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:58.060
In 2018, researchers tested face tools

43
00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:00.820
that guess gender, using photos of

44
00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:02.680
members of parliament from Rwanda,

45
00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:05.760
Senegal, South Africa and northern Europe.

46
00:02:06.390 --> 00:02:08.730
The tools were wrong for up to about one

47
00:02:08.730 --> 00:02:11.950
in three darker-skinned women, but under

48
00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:13.870
one in 100 lighter-skinned men.

49
00:02:14.490 --> 00:02:15.710
Within months, the

50
00:02:15.710 --> 00:02:17.130
companies improved them.

51
00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:19.680
How do we fix a gap?

52
00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:22.430
Collect examples from the people and

53
00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:24.290
places where a tool will be used.

54
00:02:24.990 --> 00:02:26.310
Test it with different groups.

55
00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:28.570
Let people report mistakes,

56
00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:30.530
and keep humans checking.

57
00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:33.580
Groups like Masakhane — “we build

58
00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:35.220
together” — are collecting

59
00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:36.640
African-language examples.

60
00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:41.260
After the video, try it: train a tiny

61
00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:44.220
scam spotter with English messages, then

62
00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:46.060
test it with a Kiswahili scam.

63
00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:49.360
Watch what it guesses — and ask why.

64
00:02:50.630 --> 00:02:51.370
Your turn.

65
00:02:52.010 --> 00:02:54.030
Voice typing makes more

66
00:02:54.030 --> 00:02:55.590
mistakes with my accent.

67
00:02:56.390 --> 00:02:58.330
Which fix helps most?

68
00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:01.740
Voices like Salma’s — yes!

69
00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:05.480
AI is only as good as its examples.

70
00:03:06.250 --> 00:03:08.410
Gaps often mean more mistakes

71
00:03:08.410 --> 00:03:09.850
for the people left out.

72
00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:12.040
It’s not just how many

73
00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:13.940
examples — it’s whose.

74
00:03:15.430 --> 00:03:17.950
Try it in class tomorrow: pupils draw

75
00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:20.670
fruit to “train” a pretend recogniser.

76
00:03:21.290 --> 00:03:23.530
Then show a local fruit nobody drew.

77
00:03:24.230 --> 00:03:24.950
What was missing?

78
00:03:25.730 --> 00:03:27.870
And how is that like voice

79
00:03:27.870 --> 00:03:29.010
typing and accents?
